WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.480
There aren't many graveyards in San Francisco.

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.639
Two, actually:

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:06.260
a small one at Mission Dolores Church,

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.600
and a military one at the Presidio.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.310
San Francisco land is too expensive to be
given to the dead,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.750
and it has been for a long time:

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.340
in the first decades of the 20th century,

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:20.470
all the 150,000 bodies buried in San Francisco
were moved.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.400
To one town.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.400
Here.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.580
Welcome to Colma, just a few miles south of
San Francisco:

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.279
a town known as the City of Souls.

00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:30.830
Colma is only a small place,

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.979
but about three-quarters of it is made up
of cemeteries.

00:00:33.979 --> 00:00:36.129
About one and a half thousand people live
here...

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:40.879
along with about one and a half million who
don't so much live here as just... rest.

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:44.749
That means the dead outnumber the living by
around a thousand to one.

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:47.879
And many of those bodies were moved here from
their first resting place,

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:50.940
or their second, or their third.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.620
The trouble was that San Francisco ran out
of room,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.190
and banned new burials.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.079
the dead don't pay rent,

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:59.589
and their living relatives don't really want
to either.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:02.889
With no new, er, customers arriving in San
Francisco,

00:01:02.889 --> 00:01:06.370
the old graveyards there had no money for
upkeep, and fell into ruin.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.940
There were reports of grave robbery,

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.890
of valuables or -- occasionally -- of skeletons.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:17.070
So fourteen cemetery associations founded
this town as a place for the dead.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.000
Many of those moved here were placed in mass
graves,

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.170
their tombstones sold to be used for construction
material

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.430
or dumped in the Bay as breakwaters.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:25.930
With no-one to pay for new funeral plots

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.220
and more than a hundred thousand bodies to
move,

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.270
the folks doing the job took shortcuts.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.950
Sometimes, modern construction work in San
Francisco

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:37.130
will exhume dozens of bodies that the movers
missed.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.660
So why go to the trouble of moving them at
all, then?

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.840
Well, it was either move the bodies immediately,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.370
or deal with them while you're digging foundations
for new buildings anyway.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.330
What else were they going to do?

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:52.060
Almost everyone has a fear and disgust response
to death, for good reasons:

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.950
and religion and tradition in America, then,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.030
dictated that the dead must be buried.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.810
The Catholic Church wouldn't allow cremation
until decades later,

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.700
and, well, there wasn't another option.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:03.970
And sure, the comments on this video

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:06.780
will make all sorts of horror movie or zombie
apocalypse jokes --

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:07.950
and if you've already done that,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.409
congratulations, you're not original --

00:02:09.409 --> 00:02:12.970
but here's a different question:

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.750
most people figure that cemetery plots are
forever.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.790
If you choose to be buried,

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.980
the spot where you're laid to rest is permanently
yours,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.799
at least in some sense of yours.

00:02:22.799 --> 00:02:26.230
That's not true: already, some areas of Europe
are re-using graves,

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.409
and eventually every headstone's going to
be weathered away.

00:02:28.409 --> 00:02:30.849
So here's my question:

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:34.999
when does moving a body stop being disrespect
for the dead

00:02:34.999 --> 00:02:36.419
and start being archaeology?

